  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Hard freeze coming for the mornings.\n\n- Valley fog and stratus will be common early this week.\n\n- A warming and drying trend this week.\n\n- Unsettled weather on the way for early next week.\n\nSatellite imagery early this afternoon indicates a closed\ncirculation of low pressure over eastern Oregon and southwestern\nIdaho. This system, responsible for sending Pacific moisture\nnorthwards into southwest Montana and north-central Idaho, will\ntake a more southern route going into Tuesday. It will take\nprecipitation with it, leaving a positively tilted ridge of high\npressure over the Northern Rockies. That ridge will strengthen\nthroughout the week, bringing daytime temperatures up.\nTemperatures will peak on Saturday at about 10 degrees above\nnormal.  \n\nIn the meantime, expect cold mornings with hard freezes likely in\nthe valleys of western Montana and much of north-central Idaho. \nThose in the lower valleys of the Salmon and Clearwater rivers \nshould still expect freezing temperatures, though only a few \ndegrees off the freezing mark. Valley fog will be another hallmark\nof the ridge-following-snow scenario. Morning motorists and\naviators should expect fog formation in the valleys.  \n\nEnsembles are starting to agree on another low pressure system by\nearly next week. Though most solutions are on board with a \ndisturbance, some members bring the low south, some keep it as an \nopen wave, and some show a closed low. At the current time \nhorizon, we'll let the reader chose their own adventure.",
